# requirements.in — direct third-party dependencies only. Edit this, then
# recompile the locked requirements.txt with:
#
#   uv pip compile requirements.in --override requirements-private.txt \
#     -c requirements.txt --generate-hashes --python-version 3.13 \
#     -o requirements.full.txt
#   grep -vE '^(byteforge-aegis-models|byteforge-loki-logging) @ git\+' \
#     requirements.full.txt > requirements.txt && rm requirements.full.txt
#
# Pins and hashes for the full transitive tree live in requirements.txt.
#
# The `-c requirements.txt` is load-bearing and was missing until 2026-07-29.
# uv preserves existing pins by reading its OUTPUT file — and the output here
# is requirements.full.txt, which never exists at the start of a run. So every
# recompile silently re-resolved the whole tree: adding one package also moved
# 13 unrelated pins, gunicorn among them, across a major version. Anchoring to
# the current lock means a recompile adds only what the new entry needs.
#
# Versions still move deliberately with --upgrade or --upgrade-package NAME.
#
# First-party libs (byteforge-aegis-models, byteforge-loki-logging) are
# listed here so the compile locks their third-party sub-tree, but they are
# stripped from the lock and installed at HEAD from requirements-private.txt
# instead — they deliberately float to latest.

Flask
Flask-CORS
Flask-Limiter[redis]
psycopg2-binary
bcrypt
python-dotenv
email-validator
marshmallow
requests
gunicorn

byteforge-aegis-models @ git+https://github.com/jmazzahacks/byteforge-aegis-models.git
byteforge-loki-logging @ git+https://github.com/jmazzahacks/byteforge-loki-logging.git
